{
  "rules": [
    "Late-horizon upside bias: forecasts increasingly underpredict strong rallies in the last ~5–10 days (missed breakout/acceleration). Apply an upward drift adjustment when recent outcomes show rising momentum.",
    "Down-move overshoot: during/after selloffs the model tends to forecast too low (too bearish) around the trough window (~days 15–22), while realized prices stabilize slightly higher. Damp downside amplitude in mid-horizon dips.",
    "Turning-point lag: the model is slow to recognize regime shifts (from range-bound to trending up). If the last 3–5 realized days are making higher highs/higher lows, reduce mean-reversion and increase trend-following in the forecast tail.",
    "Volatility compression: forecast paths are too smooth versus realized volatility; they understate late-stage volatility expansion. Widen forecast bands and allow larger positive deviations after an upside break.",
    "Horizon-dependent correction: keep days 1–10 close to model (errors small), apply mild correction days 11–20 (reduce dip depth), and strongest correction days 21–30 (add positive drift/convexity when trend is up)."
  ]
}
